.TH "UBERTOOTH\-SCAN" "1" "October 2026" "Project Ubertooth" "User Commands"
.SH "NAME"
\fBubertooth\-scan\fR \- active (BlueZ) device scan and inquiry supported by Ubertooth
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
ubertooth\-scan [\-s] [\-x]
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
This tool uses a normal Bluetooth dongle to perform Inquiry Scans and Extended Inquiry scans of Bluetooth devices\. It uses Ubertooth to discover undiscoverable devices and can use BlueZ to scan for discoverable devices\.
.P
The Ubertooth capture is gated by the board's squelch, which this tool does not set: it is the default \-120 dBm unless \fBubertooth\-util \-z\fR set another value while the board was idle, and the firmware resets it on every mode change, so it never carries over from an earlier capture\. See ubertooth\-util(1), SQUELCH\.
.SH "EXAMPLES"
Use Ubertooth to discover devices and perform Inquiry Scan with BlueZ:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-scan
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
Use BlueZ and Ubertooth to discover devices and perform Inquiry Scan and Extended Inquiry Scan:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-scan \-s \-x
.fi
.IP "" 0
.SH "OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-s\fR
hci Scan \- use BlueZ to scan for discoverable devices
.TP
\fB\-x\fR
eXtended scan \- retrieve additional information about target devices
.TP
\fB\-t <seconds>\fR
scan Time \- length of time to sniff packets\. [Default: 20s]
.TP
\fB\-e <0\-5>\fR
Maximum Access Code Errors (default: 2)
.TP
\fB\-b hciN\fR
Bluetooth device (default: hci0)
.TP
\fB\-U <index|serial>\fR
select an Ubertooth by enumeration index or serial number; see ubertooth(7), SELECTING A DEVICE
.TP
\fB\-h\fR
print the usage text on standard output and exit 0\. It takes effect once the whole command line has parsed, so a bad option value, a stray word or an option given twice is still reported\.
.SH "ARGUMENTS"
\fBubertooth\-scan\fR takes options only\. A word that is not an option, including a bare \fB\-\fR, is refused before any device is opened and named: \fBerror: unexpected argument '<word>'\fR, with exit status 1\.
.P
\fB\-h\fR ends the command only once the whole command line has parsed, so a stray word or a bad option value is reported even alongside it\.
.P
An option the tool does not know is refused the same way, as one line on standard error with no usage text after it: \fBerror: unrecognized option '\-Z'\fR\. An option whose value is missing reads \fBerror: option '\-t' requires a value\fR\.
.P
An option value must be a whole value in its range, or it is refused the same way instead of being read as 0, for example \fBerror: maximum access code errors must be 0\-5 (got 6)\fR\.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
A scan that ran to its \fB\-t\fR timeout, or that was interrupted (Ctrl\-C), exits 0 with whatever it found, and so does \fB\-h\fR\. A refused command line exits 1 (see ARGUMENTS)\.
.P
A scan whose USB stream fails part\-way also exits 1: the board stopped answering, or dropped off the bus, so the Ubertooth half of the scan stops there\. The results it did get are still printed, and the failure is reported on standard error as it happens, as \fBerror: receive transfer failed: <reason>\fR\. A board that never hands the transfer back at all exits 1 too, reported by the \fBwarning:\fR line ubertooth(7) describes rather than that error\.
.P
A BlueZ adapter that is not there, or that is there but will not open, exits 1 with one \fBerror:\fR line on standard error naming the adapter and why, for example \fBerror: Bluetooth device hci7 not found: No such device\fR\. Nothing is printed on standard output, so a caller collecting the scan results in a file does not find the failure among them\.
.P
A capture that could not be started at all exits 1 as well, whichever step failed: the receive transfer libusb would not take, the host thread that collects the capture, or the request that puts the board in its capture mode\. Standard output holds whatever a \fB\-s\fR inquiry printed before that and nothing else; standard error carries the one \fBerror:\fR line for the step that failed\. The capture request is named the way the sweep request below is, with the reason the board gave and the libusb code\. The status is 1 in every case, never the failing call's own return value: a negative libusb code handed to the exit status is wrapped into a byte, so \fBlibusb \-9\fR reached a script as 247\.
.P
A board that refuses the request that puts it in sweep mode exits 1 as well\. Standard output holds whatever a \fB\-s\fR inquiry printed before that and nothing else, not even the heading the Ubertooth results would have gone under\. Standard error names the request, why the board refused it and the libusb code, and adds a note where there is something to try:
.IP "" 4
.nf
error: could not set sweep mode: the board did not answer in time (libusb \-7)
note: if a mode is running, stop it with ubertooth\-util \-S; a board that does not answer at all usually resets itself within a few seconds, so run the command again, and unplug it or power\-cycle its hub port if it stays silent
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
That pair is the report\. A sweep the board's firmware has not got is stalled, which reads \fBerror: could not set sweep mode: the board's firmware does not support it (stalled, libusb \-9)\fR, with a note naming the board's firmware API version and the one these tools need (see ubertooth(7), FIRMWARE OF ANOTHER API VERSION)\. The AFH map this tool prints is only meaningful while the board sweeps the whole band, so no scan results are printed after that\. The sweep is refused with the capture already running, so the mode is stopped on the way out, and a board that has stopped answering altogether can add a line of its own to the report as that is done\.
.P
The sweep is stated to the board after the capture has been asked for, because a mode start resets the board's hop mode and would throw away a sweep stated before it: without that, a scan listened to one channel and printed a map that meant nothing\. See ubertooth\-util(1), CARRY\-OVER BETWEEN RUNS\. A board that was already capturing \(em what a killed \fBubertooth\-afh\fR or \fBubertooth\-follow\fR leaves behind \(em does that reset while it takes the capture request, so the sweep stated after it survives\. A board left in a mode that captures nothing, a spectrum scan say, resets as it leaves that mode, a moment after the capture request has been answered, and can still swallow the sweep: stop such a board with \fBubertooth\-util \-S\fR before scanning\.
.P
A BlueZ inquiry asked for with \fB\-s\fR runs before the sweep is stated, so its results are printed even when the sweep is then refused: that half of the scan needs no Ubertooth, and what it reported is what it saw\.
